    "source": "commonsensedog knowledge/Leaky Gut — The Barrier and How to Rebuild It.md",
    "contentHtml": "<h1>Leaky Gut — the barrier, and how to rebuild it</h1>\n<p>The lining of your dog&#39;s gut is <strong>one cell thick</strong>. That single layer has to absorb everything he needs while blocking everything he doesn&#39;t — toxins, bacteria, undigested food. It&#39;s the thinnest and busiest border in the body.</p>\n<p>When that seal breaks, the problem stops looking like a gut problem. It starts looking like skin, joints, mood, and &quot;he&#39;s just not right.&quot;</p>\n<h2>The gatekeeper</h2>\n<p><strong>A healthy gut is a fine cheesecloth.</strong> Fine enough to let nutrients through, tight enough to stop everything else. It works as customs, not as a wall.</p>\n<p><strong>Tight junctions are the glue.</strong> Those single-layer cells are held together by tight junctions, regulated by a protein called <strong>zonulin</strong>. When zonulin behaves, the seal is impermeable. Remember that word — it&#39;s the mechanism, and it&#39;s also how the problem gets measured.</p>\n<h2>What breaks the seal</h2>\n<p><strong>Highly processed kibble.</strong> High starch feeds the wrong organisms and gives the lining nothing to rebuild with. It&#39;s the constant low-grade version of the assault rather than a dramatic one.</p>\n<p><strong>Glyphosate.</strong> It behaves like an antibiotic in the body and directly disrupts tight junctions. The common route in is non-GMO crops sprayed at harvest to dry them — oats, peas, chickpeas, lentils. Which is why grain-free foods are so often heavily exposed.</p>\n<p><strong>Omega-6 and inflammatory fats.</strong> Surplus omega-6 keeps the inflammatory machinery running with nothing to fight, and inflammation is what makes cells swell and junctions pull apart.</p>\n<p><strong>Chronic stress.</strong> Fight-or-flight halts digestion and promotes gut inflammation and permeability. That includes <em>your</em> stress — dogs read it, and it lands in their gut.</p>\n<p><strong>Medications — the carpet-bombing effect.</strong> Antibiotics, NSAIDs, steroids and acid reducers all damage either the lining or the flora that maintain it. Sometimes necessary. But each course has a cost, and it&#39;s better to plan the repair than discover it six months later.</p>\n<h2>The breach, mechanically</h2>\n<p><strong>Inflammation creates the leak.</strong> Inflamed cells swell, tight junctions break, and holes open in a barrier meant to be sealed.</p>\n<p><strong>Then comes unauthorised entry.</strong> Large undigested proteins and environmental chemicals bypass the barrier and enter the bloodstream, sounding an alarm for the entire immune system.</p>\n<p><strong>And the immune system remembers.</strong> It logs those leaked proteins as invaders and mounts a histamine attack next time it meets them. That&#39;s the mechanism behind a dog who seems allergic to everything — <strong>it isn&#39;t the food, it&#39;s that the food is arriving somewhere it was never meant to be.</strong> Which is also why elimination diets can chase protein after protein without ever fixing anything.</p>\n<h2>Why it doesn&#39;t look like a gut problem</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Where it shows</th>\n<th>What you see</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Digestion</strong></td>\n<td>Gas, bloating, reflux, chronic loose stool — the minority of cases</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Skin and ears</strong></td>\n<td>Itching, hot spots, recurring ear infections, corn-chip smell, sticky ears, thickened skin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Joints</strong></td>\n<td>Aching and stiffness — systemic inflammation doesn&#39;t stay where it started</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Thyroid</strong></td>\n<td>Chronic immune activation doesn&#39;t respect organ boundaries</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Behaviour</strong></td>\n<td>Anxiety, reactivity, aggression. The gut–brain axis is a real signalling pathway</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>&quot;ADR&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><em>Ain&#39;t Doing Right</em> — the actual term vets use for a dog who&#39;s off but won&#39;t localise</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Yeast is a gut problem wearing a skin costume.</strong> An imbalanced microbiome lets yeast flourish and push through the skin. Treating the skin while the gut stays broken is why it keeps coming back.</p>\n<h2>Working it out at home</h2>\n<p><strong>Start a health journal.</strong> Date, food, stool, itching, energy, ear smell. Subtle changes are the whole game, and you cannot hold six weeks of gradual improvement in your head. Without a written baseline, every check-in becomes a fresh worry instead of a comparison.</p>\n<p><strong>Change one thing at a time.</strong> A protein change and a new supplement in the same week means neither result tells you anything.</p>\n<p><strong>Lab markers, if you want them.</strong> Zonulin levels can be measured as a marker of permeability, and anti-gliadin antibodies are the other one. Neither is required to start — the diet work is identical either way.</p>\n<h2>The healing path, in order</h2>\n<p><strong>1 · Species-appropriate diet.</strong> Starve the yeast, seal the gut. Low-starch, fresh, whole-food, and get the glyphosate-sprayed legumes and grains out. This step does most of the work, and skipping to supplements without it is why protocols stall at week three.</p>\n<p><strong>2 · Bone broth and colostrum.</strong> Bone broth is rich in collagen and amino acids and helps seal the junctions directly. Colostrum supports tissue repair and immune balance. These are foods rather than supplements, which is why they come early and cost little.</p>\n<p><strong>3 · Targeted repair.</strong> L-glutamine is the preferred fuel of the cells lining the gut — it&#39;s literally what they rebuild with. Zinc carnosine helps restore the lining. This is the repair toolkit, and it matters most <em>after</em> the assault has stopped, not during it.</p>\n<p><strong>4 · Soothing botanicals.</strong> Mucilaginous herbs form a protective jelly-like layer over irritated tissue, lowering inflammation so healing can happen underneath. Short-term cover, not a permanent resident — a dog who needs them daily to hold a normal stool has an unanswered question, not a supplement requirement.</p>\n<h2>The repair toolkit, by job</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Category</th>\n<th>Options</th>\n<th>What it&#39;s for</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Lining &amp; sealants</strong></td>\n<td>Bone broth · L-glutamine · NAG</td>\n<td>Rebuild the physical cell wall — structural repair, not symptom relief</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Inflammation</strong></td>\n<td>Turmeric · ginger · quercetin</td>\n<td>Quell the systemic immune fire so the barrier gets a chance to close</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Microbiome</strong></td>\n<td>S. boulardii · soil-based probiotics</td>\n<td>Displace pathogens without over-colonising. S. boulardii is a yeast, so it survives antibiotics</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Detox support</strong></td>\n<td>Humic &amp; fulvic acid · milk thistle</td>\n<td>Protect against glyphosate, and support the liver that processes whatever the gut lets through</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2>The honest timeline</h2>\n<p>This is weeks to months, not days. The barrier is a physical structure being rebuilt from raw materials — that takes as long as it takes. Most people stop somewhere around week three, decide it didn&#39;t work, and go back to managing symptoms.</p>\n<p><strong>And when to stop doing this alone:</strong> blood in the stool, sustained weight loss, a dog going genuinely off food, or vomiting that doesn&#39;t settle. Those need a vet, not a longer supplement list.</p>\n"
